lesson: 142
title: Multiplying by a one-digit number
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: To multiply a big number by a one-digit number, multiply the ones, the tens and the hundreds apart and add: 136 × 4 = 100 × 4 + 30 × 4 + 6 × 4 = 400 + 120 + 24 = 544.
explain: In writing, start with the ones and carry the tens: 6 × 4 = 24, write 4 and carry 2; 3 × 4 = 12, and 12 + 2 = 14, write 4 and carry 1; 1 × 4 = 4, and 4 + 1 = 5.
explain: The cost is the price times the quantity: 4 chairs at 136 dollars each cost 136 × 4 = 544 dollars.
vars: a=name n=13..240 k=3..9
need: n * k <= 999
need: n % 10 != 0
answer: n * k
exercise: A box holds {n} pencils. How many pencils are there in {k} boxes?
exercise: One chair costs {n} dollars. The school buys {k} chairs. How much does the school pay?
exercise: A train has {k} carriages with {n} seats in each. How many seats are there on the train?
exercise: {a} rides {n} km on {a.his} bike every week. How many kilometres does {a.he} ride in {k} weeks?
exercise: A book has {n} pages. How many pages are there in {k} copies of this book?
exercise: A bed for the summer camp costs {n} dollars. How much do {k} such beds cost?
exercise: A farmer puts {n} kg of apples into each of {k} crates. How many kilograms of apples are in the crates?
test: What do {k} bikes cost if the price of one bike is {n} dollars?
test: There are {k} rows of seats in a hall, and each row has {n} seats. How many people can sit in the hall?
test: The way from {a}'s home to school is {n} metres. How many metres does {a.he} walk on the way to school in {k} days?
test: Each of {k} classes collected {n} kg of paper. How much paper was collected in all?
exercise: How many pencils are there in {k} boxes, if one box holds {n} pencils?
exercise: {k} boxes, {n} pencils per box. How many pencils in total?
exercise: A school in Texas buys {k} desks at {n} dollars apiece. How much does it spend in all?
exercise: {a} cycles {n} km every month. How many kilometres does {a.he} cycle in {k.words} months?
exercise: A ferry in Sydney carries {n} people on each trip. How many people does it carry in {k} trips?
exercise: Every crate holds {n} kg of oranges. What is the weight of {k} crates, in kilograms?
exercise: A school in Manchester orders {k} boxes of paper, each with {n} sheets. How many sheets does it get altogether?
exercise: The price of one tent in Alberta is {n} dollars. How many dollars do {k} tents cost combined?
test: {n} seats are in every carriage of a train in Scotland. Find the seats in {k} carriages.
test: {a} reads {n} pages every month. How many pages does {a.he} read over {k} months?
test: Scooters at a shop in Ohio are {n} dollars each. What is the total price of {k} of them?
test: A truck in Queensland makes {k} trips, carrying {n} kg of sand each trip. How much sand does it carry altogether?
test: In a stadium in Cork, each of {k} sections holds {n} fans. How many fans fit into all the sections?
test: {a} saves {n} cents a week. How many cents has {a.he} saved after {k} weeks?
exercise: box holds {n} pencils, how many in {k} boxes
exercise: one chair's {n} dollars, school gets {k+1} — no wait, {k} chairs. how much
exercise: my sister says {n} × {k} is {n+k}. nah. whats the real answer
exercise: train's got {k} carriages, {n} seats each, and like 2 toilets lol. how many seats
test: {k} buses, {n} people each, how many people
test: {n} bucks a bike, {k} bikes, total?
test: {a} reckons {k} lots of {n} is {n*(k-1)}. nah. whats the answer
exam: It is given that each box contains {n} pencils. Determine the aggregate number of pencils contained in {k} such boxes.
exam: Whereas a single chair is priced at {n} dollars, ascertain the total expenditure incurred by a school in the purchase of {k.words} chairs.
exam: A train comprises {k} carriages, each furnished with {n} seats. State the total seating capacity of the train.
exam: Compute the product of {n} and {k}.
exercise: Since Dad was let go, Mum works an extra shift that brings in {n} dollars a week. How many extra dollars does it bring in over {k} weeks?
exercise: Great-aunt Rose passed away and left each of her {k} nieces {n} dollars. How many dollars did she leave them altogether?
test: money's a bit tight, so {a} flogs {k} old games at {n} dollars each — no haggling, cheapskates. how much does {a.he} make
